'''trinket<sup>1</sup>''' (tring′ket), ''v. i.'' [Formerly sometimes ''trinquet''; < ''trinket''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] To deal in a small, selfish way; hold secret communication; have private intercourse; intrigue; traffic.

{{smaller block|Had the Popish Lords stood to the interest of the Crown, . . . and not ''trinketed'' with the enemies of that and themselves, it is probable they had kept their seats in the House of Lords for many years longer.
{{right|''Roger North'', Examen, p. 63. (''Davies.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|Mysell am not clear to ''trinket'' and traffic wi' courts o' justice, as they are now constituted; I have a tenderness and scruple in my mind anent them.
{{right|''Scott'', Heart of Mid-Lothian, xviii.}}}}

'''trinket<sup>2</sup>†''' (tring′ket), ''n.'' [Perhaps < W. ''tranced'', a cup with a handle, appar. confused with ''drink'', or with OF. ''trinquer'' = It. ''trincare'', drink, quaff, carouse, < MHG. G. ''trinken'', drink: see ''drink''.] A vessel to drink or eat out of. See the quotations.

{{smaller block|''Trinket;'' a Porringer. ''Ray'', Eng. Words (ed. 1691), p. 125.}}

{{smaller block|Mrs. Bargrave asked her whether she would drink some tea. Says Mrs. Veal, I do not care if I do; but I'll warrant you, this mad fellow (meaning Mrs. Bargrave's husband) has broke all your ''trinkets.'' But, says Mrs. Bargrave, I'll get something to drink in for all that.
{{right|''Defoe'', True Relation of the Apparition of One Mrs. [Veal . . . to One Mrs. Bargrave.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|The ''trinket'' and the mizen were rent asunder.
{{right|''Hakluyt's Voyages'', III. 411.}}}}

{{smaller block|A small Sayle of a Shippe, called the ''Trinkette'', or foresayle, which is most properly the toppe-sayle of all the Shippe.
{{right|''Minsheu'' (1617).}}}}

{{smaller block|Sir W. C. writes from Brussels that the French . . . made account to have kept a brave Christmas here at London, and for that purpose had trussed up their ''trinkets'' half topmast high.
{{right|''Court and Times of Charles I.'', II. 208.}}}}

'''trinket'''<sup>4</sup> (tring′ket), ''n.'' [Appar. for *''trinklet'', < ''trinkle''<sup>1</sup> + ''-et''; a var. of ''tricklet''.] A streamlet. [Prov. Eng. and Irish.]

{{smaller block|''Trinket'' . . . is used about Dublin, and also in the northern counties, with the sense of "a little stream or watercourse by the roadside."
{{right|''N. and Q.'', 7th ser., VI. 372.}}}}

'''trinketer''' (tring′ket-ėr), ''n.'' [< ''trinket''<sup>1</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who trinkets, traffics, or intrigues, or carries on secret petty dealing.

{{smaller block|I have possessed this honourable gentleman with the full injustice which he has done and shall do to his own soul, if he becomes thus a ''trinketer'' with Satan.
{{right|''Scott'', Kenilworth, ix.}}}}

'''trinketry''' (tring′ket-ri), ''n.'' [< ''trinket''<sup>1</sup> + ''-(e)ry''.] Trinkets collectively.

{{smaller block|The Moor, who had a little taste for ''trinketry'', made out to get into his heap the most of the pearls and precious stones, and other baubles.
{{right|''Irving'', Alhambra, p. 314.}}}}

'''trinkle'''<sup>1</sup> (tring′kl), ''v. i.''; pret. and pp. ''trinkled'', ppr. ''trinkling''. [< late ME. ''trinklen''; appar. a nasalized var. of ''trickle'', prob. due to confusion with ''trintle'', ''trindle''.] 1. To trickle. ''Halliwell.'' [Obsolete or dialectal.]

{{smaller block|Ouer all his body furth ȝet the swete thik,
Lyke to the ''trynkland'' blak stremes of pik.
{{right|''Gavin Douglas'', tr. of Virgil, p. 307.}}}}
